---

<BaseLayout
  title="SHU vs Capsaicin Content: What's the Difference | Tenda Peppers"
  description="Capsaicin is the chemical that burns; SHU is the scale that measures capsaicinoid concentration — roughly 1 ppm capsaicin ≈ 16 SHU. What each means on a specification."
  ogType="article"
  author="Tenda Peppers Technical Team"
  datePublished="2026-09-23"
  dateModified="2026-09-23"
  jsonLd={[articleJsonLd, faqJsonLd]}

>
  <div class="container section">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" style="font-size:0.9rem;color:#6b5a54;margin-bottom:16px;">
      <a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/guides/">Guides</a> &rsaquo; SHU vs Capsaicin
    </nav>

    <h1>SHU vs Capsaicin Content: What's the Difference</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Capsaicin is the chemical compound that causes the burn; SHU is the scale that quantifies it. This comparison explains the relationship — and what SHU does and does not tell you about how a chilli will burn.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Capsaicin is the dominant member of the capsaicinoid family — the alkaloids that trigger the burning sensation. SHU quantifies their concentration: HPLC results are converted to SHU, with roughly 1 ppm capsaicin ≈ 16 SHU — which is why pure capsaicin registers around 16 million SHU.
    </div>
    <AtAGlance facts={glanceFacts} />

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The core difference</h2>
    <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:8px;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #d8c9c0;text-align:left;">
          <th style="padding:8px;">Dimension</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">Capsaicin</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">SHU</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>What it is</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">The dominant member of the capsaicinoid family of alkaloids</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">A concentration scale for capsaicinoids, primarily capsaicin</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Role</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Triggers the burning sensation</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Quantifies it — a chemical concentration, not a taste strength</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Conversion</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Roughly 1 ppm capsaicin ≈ 16 SHU</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Pure capsaicin ≈ 16 million SHU</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Limit</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">One compound among several</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Averages several capsaicinoids — same SHU can burn differently</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Why the choice matters</h2>
    <p>
      "Capsaicin" is often used loosely to mean "heat", but it is really the dominant member of a small family of alkaloids. The full background is on the <a href="/guides/asta-colour-and-shu-heat-explained/">SHU explained</a> page. Two chillies can share an identical SHU yet burn at a different speed, linger for a different time, and taste entirely different — because the number averages several capsaicinoids whose sensory character differs.
    </p>
    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What SHU does not tell you</h2>
    <p>
      SHU is a chemical concentration, not a taste strength. Perceived heat is a sensory response that depends on the capsaicinoid mix, the food matrix and the individual taster — measured SHU and perceived heat correlate but are not interchangeable.
    </p>
    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">How to specify</h2>
    <p>
      Specify heat as an SHU window, not a single number — capsaicinoid content can shift ±40% within a single variety with growing conditions.
    </p>
    <p class="data-note">
      Heat and colour values are representative. Confirm your exact SHU window, ASTA, form and mesh
      when you inquire.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is the difference between SHU and capsaicin?</h3>
      <p>Capsaicin is the chemical compound — the dominant capsaicinoid that triggers the burn. SHU is the scale that measures capsaicinoid concentration: roughly 1 ppm capsaicin ≈ 16 SHU.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is pure capsaicin in SHU?</h3>
      <p>Pure capsaicin registers around 16 million SHU — the conversion of roughly 1 ppm capsaicin ≈ 16 SHU.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Does the same SHU always burn the same?</h3>
      <p>No. Two chillies can share an identical SHU yet burn at a different speed and linger for a different time, because the number averages several capsaicinoids whose sensory character differs.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top:32px;">
      <a class="cta" href="/guides/asta-colour-and-shu-heat-explained/">Read the SHU Guide</a>
      <a class="cta cta-outline" href="/contact/" style="margin-left:8px;">Request a Quote</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
